From bc7051860ecc5b33300409c47e1b74e9767a7e6b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tm <tm@x>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:55:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 35/36] let inlined: a lambda called at the head of a form is
 compiled in the frame of the function

From Julia's femtolisp (5e949d7915). ((lambda (v ...) body) e ...), the
expansion of let, made a closure and called it (since the previous
patch, a closure of the variables it uses). When the lambda has as many
plain variables as there are values, the values are now pushed on the
stack and are variables of the frame of the function (loada, box), the
body is compiled there, and the new instruction shift drops the values
under the result (in tail position, ret or tcall does). The compiler
follows the depth of the stack (bcode:sp, bcode:stack) to know the index
of these variables, and complex-bindings treats the body of such a
lambda as code of the function, not of a closure. loada and seta no
longer assert that the function has arguments.

TeXmacs adaptations: the depth of the stack is counted in the code of the
patches (n-ary < and =, the calls of more than 255 arguments, trycatch);
in the default values of the optional arguments the depth is not known
(more arguments than variables, before vargc), bcode:sp is #f there and
the lambdas are called, not inlined (Julia's femtolisp started at 0 and
got the indices wrong).

Inlined variables are not in the stack traces (only the arguments).
Boot image regenerated with the cross step (mkboot0.lsp --compiler).
